mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p><span style="font-family: 'times new roman', times, serif;">Pharmaceutics is a comprehensive applied discipline focusing on the design principles, formulation processes, manufacturing techniques, quality control, and rational application of pharmaceutical preparations. It is a core component of the pharmacy curriculum. As an essential practical component of the Pharmaceutics course, Pharmaceutics&nbsp; Experiment I&nbsp; plays an irreplaceable role in integrating theoretical knowledge with practical skills. The course aims to enhance students’ experimental techniques, independent thinking, creativity, and the ability to analyze and solve problems comprehensively. The course covers the preparation processes and quality evaluation of 11 commonly used dosage forms, including liquid preparations, suspensions, and tablets, along with experimental investigations of relevant influencing factors. It enables students to systematically master the preparation methods and quality assessment principles of conventional pharmaceutical preparations.</span></p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
